    "body": "John Deere plans to close its Ottumwa, Iowa, factory and end manufacturing at its Hagie plant in Clarion, Iowa, as it moves equipment production among existing factories in the United States and Brazil.\nWhat’s Related\nThe changes will unfold over Deere’s fiscal years 2027 through 2029. Windrower and round baler production will move from Ottumwa to Deere’s Des Moines Works in Ankeny, Iowa, while Hagie STS sprayer production will move from Clarion to Thibodaux, Louisiana.\nDeere also plans to move sugar cane harvester production from Thibodaux to Catalão, Brazil, and small planter production from Moline, Illinois, to Paton, Iowa. The company cited changing customer demand and available factory space as reasons for moving production to Iowa.\nDeere told DTN that more than 300 people work at Ottumwa and more than 200 work at Clarion. The company has not announced a final number of job cuts.\nRELATED: John Deere Squashes Shutdown Rumors With $20 Billion U.S. Investment\nWhat's going where\nWindrower production will move to Des Moines Works by the end of fiscal 2027, followed by round balers by the end of fiscal 2028. Deere intends to close the Ottumwa factory once those transfers are complete.\nDeere will begin moving Hagie STS sprayers to Louisiana in fiscal 2028, with manufacturing at Clarion ending by fiscal 2029. Small planter production will move small planter production to Paton in fiscal 2027.\nSugar cane harvester production will shift to Brazil in fiscal 2029. Deere already makes the machines in Catalão and said the move will bring production closer to its largest concentration of customers in South America.\nThe Louisiana factory currently produces about 35 sugar cane harvesters annually for the U.S. market. Deere told DTN it does not expect the change to reduce headcount at Thibodaux because sprayer production will move there.\nMore sourcing changes may be coming. Deere’s Construction and Forestry division is also reviewing how it sources parts and production work to manage costs and respond to changing demand. The announcement does not identify specific changes.\nEmployment impact remains uncertain\nDeere spokesperson Dustin Lemmon told the Iowa Capital Dispatch that about 300 people would be affected at normal production levels, but the actual employment impact remains uncertain. Ottumwa city officials put the number higher, telling WQAD the closure will cost about 400 jobs and more than $30 million in payroll. Possible employee assistance includes transfers, retraining, relocation assistance, and severance benefits.\nDeere is “currently operating far below normal production volumes due to the downturn in the ag economy ,” Lemmon said.\nThe company said it will work with employees, union representatives where applicable, and local leaders during the transitions. It also plans to identify future uses for the Ottumwa and Clarion sites.\nDeere said in its announcement that it expects to keep serving customers without disruption during the moves and that it aims to maintain product quality, availability and service.",
